WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.280
Hello! In this video, I will show you the results&nbsp;
of me spending 100 hours reviewing the source code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:13.360
of Elastic applications to score some bounties&nbsp;
in Elastic bug bounty program. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:18.560
my result is not great, and if you're only&nbsp;
interested in success stories, then this video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:24.960
is not for you. But if you also want to see what I&nbsp;
did wrong, what I did well, and what I did learn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:32.240
then stay with me. Enjoy! So first two days were&nbsp;
pretty much spent on the whole setup. I wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:39.680
be able to debug Kibana to just have an edge over&nbsp;
other hunters. And setting up is never pleasurable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:46.160
activity for me. But when doing the bug bounty,&nbsp;
every time I spent some time preparing myself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:52.800
I know that I will be ahead of those hunters who&nbsp;
didn't want to invest this time now and will have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:01:00.320
worse environment for testing later. I wanted to&nbsp;
be able to debug both Kibana and Elasticsearch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:07.120
Because Kibana is an application to present data,&nbsp;
but the data is actually stored in Elasticsearch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:12.320
database. And I chose Kibana as my main&nbsp;
target, but I also wanted to be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:18.480
debug Elasticsearch. But it was quite hard&nbsp;
to to set up and at the end, I was able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:25.600
to debug Kibana but not Elasticsearch. I only&nbsp;
used Docker image for it. Then, I read some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:31.920
reports from Hackerone to know what to expect,&nbsp;
what are the weak sides of Kibana and I started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:38.720
testing. The way I would test the application is&nbsp;
I would simply start using it from the browser&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:44.800
and choose a functionality that I would like to&nbsp;
to test. And then I would start understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:50.880
what happens. I would start reading the code,&nbsp;
putting in breakpoints to see what issues can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:58.400
in this place of code. And I really enjoyed this&nbsp;
faze. Every day, I was understanding more and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:04.880
functionalities. I saw how they worked. And to be&nbsp;
honest, Kibana seemed a little bit sketchy to me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.040
You know, sometimes when you test an&nbsp;
application or look at the source code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:14.880
you just feel it's secure. That the developers&nbsp;
saw everything you can try. In this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:22.640
I felt it was written really badly. For example,&nbsp;
there are a lot of path traversals and you see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:29.520
that someone wrote this application without this&nbsp;
security issue in mind, but those traversals are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:35.840
fixed in places where user input could actually&nbsp;
reach those dangerous places. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:43.360
here is a code that takes... Kibana takes&nbsp;
the request from the user and forwards it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:49.680
to Elasticsearch. This is quite common actually.&nbsp;
And there is path traversal in this functionality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:58.640
But this input has to be an ID of an object&nbsp;
that already exists so you can't use the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:04.880
that ../ because there is no such object. But&nbsp;
there's another way that this function allows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:12.160
which is using a wildcard. And without getting&nbsp;
into details of this logic, I was able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:19.760
to prepare this payload which would both look to&nbsp;
Kibana like a wildcard, not throw an error, and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:27.600
most importantly, it would also give me the the&nbsp;
path traversal. The thing is that after writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:33.520
this, I was already happy. I thought that I will&nbsp;
submit my my first report. But it turned out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:41.120
that this function by default has allowWildcards&nbsp;
argument set to false. And there is not a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:47.600
place in Kibana source code where this function&nbsp;
is called with allowWildcards argument. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:55.200
there for a reason so it was used sometime in&nbsp;
the past, but apparently it is no not no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:02.960
vulnerable. But it was. And that pretty much&nbsp;
sums up a lot of my time spent there. So I saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:11.600
a place which looked like it's vulnerable but&nbsp;
but it wasn't. Next, after finding out that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:17.360
this particular spot is not vulnerable, I would&nbsp;
try to create a code signature, which is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:25.040
a proper search to find a similar vulnerability&nbsp;
in other place of the source code where maybe it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:33.520
would be vulnerable. Here is another example which&nbsp;
I found by looking for similar code patterns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:38.080
This is a functionality which allows you&nbsp;
to browse some external packages on Kibana.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:45.040
And those files are stored on another server - on&nbsp;
Elastic Package Registry and this is the way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:52.160
the path is created to make a request to EPR.&nbsp;
But you can see that there is no URL encoding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:59.120
so there is path traversal here. However, Elastic&nbsp;
package registry is public anyway, so there is no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:05.920
impact to this. If you could find an open redirect&nbsp;
on a package registry, it would be an SSRF.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:11.760
But I looked through the whole source code&nbsp;
of the package registry and I didn't find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:16.320
the open redirect, but if you want you&nbsp;
can give it a try. And here is another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:23.680
case where the code looks bad. There is&nbsp;
a vulnerability, but it is not exploitable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:30.960
so perhaps it's not the vulnerability after&nbsp;
all. And after a few cases like this, I started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:36.960
questioning my choice to to stick to Kibana and&nbsp;
I wanted to look for other targets. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:43.600
was very hopeful when I found out about Enterprise&nbsp;
Search application. Unlike most Elastic products,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:50.240
it is not open source. But you can install it&nbsp;
from Docker image. And you know that if you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:56.640
install something using Docker Image, you can&nbsp;
extract at least some kind of bytecode. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:02.720
wanted to extract this bytecode and maybe attack&nbsp;
this application, which is not as easy to get.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:09.920
But turns out this is how the jar files look after&nbsp;
the the decompilation. Every single file looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:16.560
exactly the same but the only difference is&nbsp;
this string or these bytes. Turns out that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:24.000
technology is called JRuby. And the way it works&nbsp;
is you have some kind of Ruby bytecode although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:31.040
bytecode isn't a valid word to describe this.&nbsp;
And it is executed by a Java program, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:38.640
problem is there are no public JRuby decompilers&nbsp;
available in the Internet. The first result that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:47.600
pops up when you Google this is a private repository from&nbsp;
GitLab, from the guy who, by no accident, is top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:53.760
one hacker on Elastic bug bounty program. And I&nbsp;
say it's no accident because I talked with him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.760 --> 00:07:00.720
and he told me that Kibana is quite hardened at&nbsp;
the moment and he finds most of most of his bugs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:06.880
on Enterprise Search due to the fact that he's&nbsp;
the only one that has the source code access to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:14.480
that. I also thought about writing the compiler&nbsp;
myself, but I decided that maybe 100 hours is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:23.440
enough for that so I ended up not doing this. And&nbsp;
those situations showed me that my hacker instincts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:29.920
are correct - I'm just too late to the party.&nbsp;
But even though I wasn't finding a lot of bugs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:35.440
you probably know that not finding bugs motivates&nbsp;
you more than finding the bugs, because then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:41.280
you try to go deeper, you try to research new&nbsp;
attack techniques... And I was learning a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:47.360
and I had quite a lot of materials to share in&nbsp;
BBRE newsletter in BBRE Premium. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:54.560
take this one from BBRE Premium #25.&nbsp;
You have these 3 regexes and think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.560 --> 00:08:00.640
which of them which will match and which of&nbsp;
these inputs. Specifically, the question is which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:09.200
regexes have escaped dots and which are wildcards.&nbsp;
The first one obviously matches wildcards, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:15.440
means that the dot is matched to any character.&nbsp;
I'm sure that you knew that already. The third one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:22.400
matches escaped dots, which means the dot&nbsp;
only matches the dot, but the middle one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:29.840
I would think it would also match a literal&nbsp;
dot, but turns out it doesn't. And in this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:36.080
the dot is the wildcard. I am not the only&nbsp;
one who doesn't know this because the Kibana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:41.680
developers don't know this either. This&nbsp;
is the code where they have badly written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:48.560
regexes. Unfortunately, to my knowledge, this is&nbsp;
not in a place which is a security vulnerability.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:54.800
And after some time, I found a bug. I was&nbsp;
able to control the source of the image.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.800 --> 00:09:01.920
It doesn't allow you to to execute XSS, but it&nbsp;
allows you to point this this URL to a logout.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:08.160
And then every single person that visits this&nbsp;
address, visits the website where your payload&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:14.640
is present, are logged out of the application. So&nbsp;
this is a functionality-level denial of service.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:20.960
It can be a medium-risk bug if it's in a critical&nbsp;
functionality, maybe even high sometimes. In this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:30.320
case it wasn't, but it was a bug. But the denial&nbsp;
of service bugs were out of scope of Elastic. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:37.280
I would probably ignore this this rule if I would&nbsp;
really find like one-click denial of service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:45.040
bug for the whole application. But in this case,&nbsp;
I decided that it is not risky enough for me to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:53.360
to ignore the policy. So I didn't report this, but&nbsp;
after a few weeks, I checked the policy again and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:59.840
turned out that they made a change, effectively&nbsp;
removing denial of service from out of scope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:05.760
vulnerabilities. So I started writing the&nbsp;
report. I confirmed that other users can also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:12.480
see my payload and I started to write the report.&nbsp;
But unfortunately, turned out that I copied the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:20.880
wrong session cookie when confirming this bug&nbsp;
and I was the only one who could see my payload&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:27.920
so I could only logout myself of the application.&nbsp;
So there is no impact to this so I ended up not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:34.560
reporting this bug, which wasn't a high risk&nbsp;
anyway - it was definitely a low risk. 

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:40.880
But I hoped that I will at least report&nbsp;
something finally, which was a bit discouraging.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:48.480
So again, I started jumping between different&nbsp;
assets of Elastic. That included Elastic cloud.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:55.760
And quickly, I found low risk vulnerability&nbsp;
there, but unfortunately it was a duplicate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.760 --> 00:11:00.240
And this time I did submit the report, but it&nbsp;
was a duplicate and judging by the report ID,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:08.000
it was a duplicate of a bug from a year before. So&nbsp;
they probably just don't care about it, which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:15.040
yet another discouraging moment. And after that,&nbsp;
I decided that I will stick with Kibana until the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:21.840
end. And I think I spent these last hours really&nbsp;
well. At the beginning, I told you that I browsed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:27.840
Hackerone for public bug bounty reports. But, of&nbsp;
course, there are not many bugs disclosed there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:34.400
But turns out that Elastic has its own bug&nbsp;
tracker and there are other vulnerabilities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:40.240
And, of course, there are no proof of concepts&nbsp;
there, but they are public. So I started to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:46.960
at a bugs from this tracker and to reverse patches&nbsp;
to these bugs to maybe find similar ones and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:53.040
understand the application better. And I think it&nbsp;
was a really good thing. It taught me a lot. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:59.040
it gave me much better understanding of the bug&nbsp;
actually than just reading the hackerone report&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:04.880
because I had to understand the whole context, I&nbsp;
had to understand the source code around this bug.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:11.280
And this gave me really good understanding.&nbsp;
For example, here's an interesting XSS which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:17.280
doesn't exist in the default configuration&nbsp;
because it is in the code written by Kibana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:23.440
but to use this code, you have to create your&nbsp;
own plugin. So there is a code in Kibana, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:31.760
have to create your own plugin and you have to use&nbsp;
one particular library, one particular function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:37.360
that Kibana gives you so this bug is actually&nbsp;
present. So you don't misuse the library,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:43.440
you use it properly and there is an XSS. So&nbsp;
what I started to do after this was I started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:51.360
to create my own plugins. It was a little bit hard&nbsp;
for me, especially that I am not TypeScript&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:57.600
developer. I can read the code but I'm not that&nbsp;
good at writing and I think I started doing this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:05.440
too late. I was able to create my plugins, but&nbsp;
it was not enough time to experiment with using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:11.520
different libraries, different use cases.&nbsp;
But it was definitely, definitely worth it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:19.840
And then finally after 92 hours, out of 100, I&nbsp;
found the valid bug. I actually found it by accident.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:27.280
I was looking for something else and I noticed&nbsp;
in the file next to the one I was inspecting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:34.080
there was a vulnerable code which I immediately&nbsp;
spotted and even though it was barely exploitable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:40.640
it was a valid bug. So I submitted it and they&nbsp;
didn't resolve it yet and didn't disclose it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:45.440
so I can't tell you what it was. From the&nbsp;
technical standpoint, it's not very interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:55.600
unfortunately. They paid me $584 for it. And&nbsp;
unfortunately, that is the final result of my&nbsp;

00:13:55.600 --> 00:14:04.000
challenge. For those of you who don't know how&nbsp;
this works, 100 hours for me is, a month of &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:10.080
full time work. So obviously, I do other&nbsp;
things which includes these videos, so it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:17.360
spread across four months for me. But if I would&nbsp;
only do bug bounty, then this challenge would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:26.400
take me one month. So that's how I compare these&nbsp;
results to one month's salary of a full time job.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:33.920
Actually, I would expect such challenge to take me&nbsp;
about two months, but this one took me almost four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:40.160
just because I procrastinated a lot, which was&nbsp;
simply caused by not finding bugs. So what are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:45.760
lessons that I learned from this challenge? The&nbsp;
first one is to not go for programs about which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:51.760
you hear from someone else because they tend to&nbsp;
be overhyped. I started hacking on Elastic because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:58.160
I heard about it on Twitter or in some talk,&nbsp;
and I see this as a mistake because if someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:03.440
mentions a program that is cool, it automatically&nbsp;
means that a lot of people are jumping on it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:09.920
making it not cool anymore. Of course, it doesn't&nbsp;
apply to huge programs like Google or Facebook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:18.320
but if I would be choosing my next program, I&nbsp;
would not go for one that someone recommends me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:24.640
And also if you ever ask someone which&nbsp;
program to choose to start bug bounty, I get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:33.040
asked this a lot, don't do this and don't go for&nbsp;
a program that someone recommends to you. But if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:38.960
I would hunt on Elastic again, what would I do&nbsp;
differently? First, it's definitely worth it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:44.640
invest time to prepare your environment, prepare&nbsp;
your tools, write your own decompiler maybe. 

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:51.840
It definitely pays off. I would also spend more&nbsp;
time trying to understand the business side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:59.120
because from the technical standpoint, of course,&nbsp;
I can understand the functionality and what Kibana is but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:08.000
I don't even know what is its place in a company.&nbsp;
What do the roles mean in real life? And also how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:13.840
does Kibana integrate with other products of
Elastic. I think I could have done a better job of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.960 --> 00:16:21.120
also learning what Logstash is. I know there&nbsp;
is the there is Beats application in scope. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:26.960
It's a whole infrastructure around&nbsp;
Elasticsearch and I probably should have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.960 --> 00:16:33.520
invested more time into understanding this and&nbsp;
maybe not choosing the most commonly hacked and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:41.040
the easiest to setup, which was Kibana.&nbsp;
And also, I think writing my own plugins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:46.320
is definitely a good way because first, you&nbsp;
start to understand the application from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:51.040
developer perspective. But also you can&nbsp;
reach the code which is not reachable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:56.560
the other way. So you are pretty sure that 
there are bugs to be found there. If you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.560 --> 00:17:02.960
try to find a bug that I've missed or maybe you&nbsp;
just want to see how I organize my notes, you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:10.080
access my whole Notion page from hunting on Elastic&nbsp;
under this URL: bbre.dev/elastic 

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:17.360
And in the next bounty vlog, challenge myself to&nbsp;
jump from different private programs on Hackerone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.360 --> 00:17:24.080
to test completely different approach. In fact,&nbsp;
I already uploaded one vulnerability that I found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.280 --> 00:17:31.840
doing this challenge. It was quite a nice SSRF,&nbsp;
which I escalated to account takeover. You can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:37.440
watch it by clicking the card which is on your&nbsp;
screen right now. And you don't want to miss the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:43.840
next bounty vlogs, subscribe to the channel!
For now, thank you for watching and goodbye!

